ext-only books, the extra cost is hard to justify. B&W E Ink has better contrast (300 ppi text vs 150 ppi color text), faster page turns, and longer battery life. If you read comics, manga with color covers, magazines, textbooks with diagrams, or want to see your library's book covers in color, the premium is worth it. The Kobo Libra Colour at $229 is the best value in 2026.</p> <p><strong>Q: How does E Ink Kaleido 3 compare to Gallery 3 color technology?</strong><br>A: Kaleido 3 is the current generation of color filter array (CFA) technology, used in the Kobo Libra Colour, Kindle Colorsoft, and Boox Go Color 7. It delivers 4096 colors at 150 ppi (color) or 300 ppi (B&W) with reasonable refresh speed. Gallery 3 is E Ink's advanced full-color electrophoretic technology that can display 50,000+ colors at higher saturation, but it has much slower refresh rates (several seconds per page turn) and is not yet found in mainstream consumer e-readers. In 2026, Kaleido 3 is the practical choice; Gallery 3 remains a lab technology for niche applications.</p>
